  "query_preview": "(Today through Sunday)...\nIssued at 316 AM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nDry and near normal conditions again today as subsidence and \nnortherly flow will continue to influence the weather across the \narea. Surface flow will start to shift to out of the south Tuesday \nas the upper high returns to the area, and bringing with it a return \nof above normal temperatures. Luckily lower dew points are expected \nwith this brief return of than was seen last week, so heat indices \nshould largely stay below to near 100. \n\nThe upper ridging will start to break down midweek while troughing \nover Canada and the northern portion of the CONUS sag into the Great \nLakes region. A surface cold front associated with the troughing \nwill bring chances for rain and storms late Wednesday through \nThursday. At this time can't rule out isolated damaging winds \nwith the line of storms, but confidence is low at this time. \n\nTemperatures will again drop back down to near normal behind the \nfront. Models are showing additional waves moving through for the \nweekend and into early next week which could bring fluctuating \ntemperatures and periodic chances for rain. Timing and details \nremain unclear at this time however.",
